%% RcAstro.pidoc
%% PIDoc source for the FlapAstro RC-Astro CLI wrapper.

\documentclass PIScriptDoc

\script { RC-Astro CLI Wrapper }

\keywords
{
   RC-Astro, BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator, NoiseXTerminator, PixInsight, deconvolution, star removal, noise reduction
}

\author
{
   FlapAstro
}

\copyright
{
   Copyright (c) 2026 FlapAstro. PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0.
}

\brief
{
   Version 0.10.2. A PixInsight V8 graphical wrapper for the stand-alone
   RC-Astro command-line tools.
}

\description
{
   RC-Astro CLI Wrapper provides a PixInsight graphical interface for the
   stand-alone BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator, and NoiseXTerminator
   command-line tools. It can process the active PixInsight view or image files
   selected manually. The documentation button beside the Preferences button
   opens this bundled compiler-generated PIDoc page.

   \subsection { Requirements }
   {
      PixInsight 1.9.5 or newer with the V8 JavaScript runtime is required.
      RC-Astro CLI version 1.0.0 or newer is required. This release has been
      tested with RC-Astro CLI version 1.1.3.
   }

   \subsection { Startup checks }
   {
      At startup, the script locates the configured RC-Astro executable and
      reads its machine-readable catalog with \c {rc-astro --json}. Versions
      older than 1.0.0 are rejected. Versions newer than 1.1.3 generate a
      warning because they were not available when this wrapper was tested.
      When the check succeeds, the main status area displays Ready together
      with the installed CLI version and executable path.
   }

   \subsection { Processing modes }
   {
      \list
      {
         {
            \s {Active PixInsight view} saves a temporary XISF copy of the
            selected view, runs RC-Astro, and opens the result as a new image.
         }
         {
            \s {Manual file} processes an input file and writes to the selected
            output path.
         }
      }

      The triangular New Instance button at the lower left creates a standard
      PixInsight process icon containing the current RC-Astro processing
      settings. Drag or click the icon onto a view to reuse those settings.
      Active-view instances obtain their image from the target or active view;
      manual-file instances also retain their input, output, and log paths.
      Machine-specific executable and temporary-directory preferences, detected
      CLI status, progress, and other transient run-time state are deliberately
      excluded. Configure machine-specific paths with the Preferences button on
      each computer.

      When a result is opened, the wrapper copies the exact screen transfer
      function (STF) of the input view. A nonidentity STF is retained for the
      usual linear-image workflow, while an identity STF remains identity for
      an already stretched nonlinear image, avoiding an unintended second
      stretch. This changes display only and never modifies output pixels.
      In manual-file mode the STF can be copied only when the selected input
      file is already open in PixInsight; otherwise the result opens with its
      default identity STF. The same rule is applied to an optional
      StarXTerminator stars-only output.
   }

   \subsection { BlurXTerminator }
   {
      Automatic nonstellar PSF is the normal mode. BlurXTerminator estimates
      the PSF from stars and can correct optical aberrations in nonstellar
      features.

      For starless, lunar, or planetary images, disable automatic nonstellar
      PSF and set the nonstellar diameter to the expected stellar FWHM in
      pixels. Manual mode assumes a round PSF and cannot correct optical
      aberrations.

      This wrapper uses the RC-Astro 1.0.0 and later parameters
      \c {--auto-nonstellar-psf} and \c {--nonstellar-diameter}. The obsolete
      radius parameters are not used.
   }

   \subsection { StarXTerminator }
   {
      The StarXTerminator panel provides optional stars-image output and
      unscreened-stars controls in addition to the common RC-Astro options.
   }

   \subsection { NoiseXTerminator }
   {
      The NoiseXTerminator panel provides controls for luminance and color
      denoising, frequency scale, and iterations. The newer ML3 model produces
      the same result with improved execution speed on Windows and Linux. The
      macOS NoiseXTerminator ML3 model was not updated with this optimization.
   }

   \subsection { Progress and cancellation }
   {
      RC-Astro output is monitored while processing. A job is stopped if the
      process produces no new output for 5 minutes. The total processing time
      is otherwise not limited. Closing the dialog during processing requests
      cancellation. Completion and failure details are shown in the main
      status area and written to the PixInsight console; successful completion
      does not display a modal popup.
   }

   \subsection { Troubleshooting }
   {
      \list
      {
         {
            \s {CLI not found:} Open Preferences and select the installed
            \c {rc-astro} executable.
         }
         {
            \s {Version check failed:} Run \c {rc-astro --json} in a terminal
            and verify that it returns a \c {cliVersion}.
         }
         {
            \s {Exit code 77:} The selected RC-Astro product is not licensed on
            this computer.
         }
         {
            \s {Missing output:} Inspect the full log and verify that the output
            directory is writable.
         }
      }
   }

   \subsection { CPD status and release requirements }
   {
      The current wrapper exports images and launches the standalone RC-Astro CLI to process them. This architecture conflicts with CPD rule 5.8 unless PTeam grants a written exception under rule 5.6. No such exception has been verified for this documentation update. A valid CPD signature identifies the signer and protects integrity; it does not establish product approval or compliance. Documentation changes do not resolve this architectural limitation.

      CPD rules version 1.0.2 were published on October 29, 2026, with an effective date of December 12, 2026. Rule 10.2 gives existing CPDs 60 days from notification to comply; the applicable notification date must be checked separately.

      Rules: https://pixinsight.com/security/cpd-rules/
   }

   \subsection { Execution and user choice }
   {
      The script launches the configured RC-Astro executable with --json at startup, before its main dialog opens, to check compatibility. If no path has been saved, it searches C:/Program Files/RC-Astro/cli/rc-astro.exe, C:/Program Files/RC-Astro/rc-astro.exe, and C:/Program Files (x86)/RC-Astro/cli/rc-astro.exe. Preferences lets the user select another executable; saving a valid selection also probes it. Successful probes are cached for the script session. Processing launches the same executable with the selected bxt, sxt, or nxt command and arguments. The wrapper does not install or license the CLI.
   }

   \subsection { Files and storage }
   {
      The default working directory is the operating-system temporary directory returned by File.systemTempDirectory. The user can change it in Preferences. Active-view mode writes VIEW_ID_rcastro_input.xisf, VIEW_ID_BXT.xisf, VIEW_ID_SXT.xisf, or VIEW_ID_NXT.xisf, and VIEW_ID_rcastro_TOOL.log in that directory. The view identifier is sanitized for filenames. Manual-file mode uses the selected input, output, and log paths. StarXTerminator can additionally produce a companion stars image beside its main output.
   }

   \subsection { Retention and overwrite }
   {
      The temporary active-view input is deleted after completion, failure, or cancellation unless Keep temporary input file is enabled. Outputs and logs are retained; there is no automatic cleanup for them. Temporary filenames are deterministic rather than unique per run. An existing temporary input at the generated path is removed before export. Overwrite is enabled by default; when enabled, existing main outputs and recognized stars-output candidates are removed before processing, so a failed run does not restore them. Use a dedicated working directory and preserve needed outputs before another run.
   }

   \subsection { Logs and preferences }
   {
      Logs contain timestamps, executable and image paths, selected command and arguments, processing status, and RC-Astro stdout/stderr. Extra command-line arguments can consequently appear in logs; do not include secrets in them. Preferences are stored through the PJSR Settings interface under RcAstroGUI/. The wrapper does not directly edit PixInsight core settings files. Review logs before sharing them.
   }

   \subsection { Result opening }
   {
      The Open result option controls opening the main output. When StarXTerminator stars output is requested and a recognized companion file is found, the current code opens that stars image even if Open result is disabled. The wrapper copies the input display STF when available without changing output pixels.
   }

   \subsection { Network and third-party behaviour }
   {
      RcAstro.js contains no direct network-transfer or telemetry calls and no executable downloader. This is a source-level statement about the wrapper, not a guarantee about the separately installed RC-Astro CLI. The CLI's network connections, licensing traffic, model downloads, cache locations, and integrity verification have not been verified here. Do not describe the combined product as offline or fully compliant on this basis. Before any release represented as CPD-compliant, verify and document those behaviours for supported CLI versions, including endpoints, purposes, storage locations, secure transport, integrity checks, and any consent requirements.
   }

